Cybersecurity matters because digital threats affect everyone, not just organizations or IT professionals. 
Awareness and basic security knowledge are essential to protect individuals from the growing impact of cyber attacks.

Many countries, including those outside India, have been victims of major cyber attacks. 
A lack of awareness is a key reason behind this.
Even in India, a large number of individuals have suffered from cyber attacks such as phishing, online fraud, and identity theft, which has led the government to issue public warnings and advisories.

I personally believe that awareness efforts need to go beyond basic warnings(which Our govt. is already trying). 
Cyber threats are a serious and ongoing issue, and they have the potential to cause significant financial, emotional, and reputational damage to individuals. 
Stronger education, continuous awareness, and proactive cybersecurity practices are essential to reduce these risks.

Its not an IT thing, Even non-technical people can learn about this. it don't require any high amount of time.

Many of us have experienced spam or fraud calls, SMS messages asking us to “update KYC,” or links claiming free vouchers and rewards. 
Because we encounter these attempts so frequently, most of us can now identify them easily and avoid falling for them.

This familiarity, however, has created a false sense of security. We are not secure, bad actors out there try different things to break our security.
They are no longer relying only on old-school methods.

Cybercriminals are becoming more sophisticated, using advanced techniques such as personalized phishing, fake but realistic websites, social engineering, and AI-generated messages that closely mimic legitimate communication.

The problem is that many victims are still mentally prepared only for outdated attack patterns. 
They expect obvious red flags, but modern attacks are designed to look normal, trustworthy, and urgent, making them harder to detect.

This is why cybersecurity awareness must evolve. People need to understand how advanced today’s attacks have become, not just how scams looked in the past. 
Awareness should focus on recognizing subtle manipulation, verifying sources, and questioning digital interactions—even when they appear genuine.

